# SYNOPSIS

```usage
uname [-amnrsv]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`uname` prints facts about the system on one line, separated by spaces, in the order of the flags below; with no flag it prints the system's name.

: `-s`
  The system's name: `VectraOS`.
: `-n`
  The node's name: `vectra`.
: `-r`
  The release: `0.1.0`.
: `-v`
  The version: `M4`.
: `-m`
  The machine: `x86_64` or `aarch64`.
: `-a`
  All of them.

The values are the C library's: `uname` asks no server for them.

`uname` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/uname`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/uname`.
There is no native `uname`.

# SEE ALSO

hostname(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/uname.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

An argument or a wrong flag prints the usage message and exits 1; a write error exits 1 too.

# BUGS

Every value is fixed when the C library is built (`ports/musl/vx/start.c`): the node's name is always `vectra`, the release and version are not the system's, and the version still says `M4`.

`uname` is untested on VectraOS.
